  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 201 AM EST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nThe actual cold front will approach South Florida on Thursday \nnight before pushing through the region on Friday morning. Chances\nfor showers out ahead of the actual front will be on the lower \nend as the prefrontal trough would have helped to reduce some of \nthe available moisture over the region. Once the front passes \nthrough, strong high pressure will begin to build into the area \nfrom the northwest. As the pressure gradient tightens, winds will \nincrease out of the northwest allowing for strong cold air \nadvection to take place beginning on Friday afternoon and lasting \ninto the first part of the weekend. This strong cold air advection\nwill allow for the coolest airmass of the fall season so far to \nmove into South Florida during this time frame. Low temperatures \non Friday night into Saturday morning have the potential to drop \ninto the lower 50s across the Lake Okeechobee region and into the \ninterior portions of Southwest Florida. Most other areas will see \nlow temperatures drop into the mid to upper 50s, while lower 60s \nwill remain possible along the immediate east coast. High \ntemperatures on Saturday will only rise into the upper 60s across \nthe Lake Okeechobee region and they will rise into the lower 70s \nacross most other areas. Low temperatures will continue to remain \ncool on Saturday night into Sunday morning, however, they may be \nvery slightly warmer as compared to the previous night due to\nwinds beginning to veer and become north northeasterly.\n\nAs high pressure continues to shift eastward dry conditions will \nremain in place for Sunday, however, with winds continuing to veer\nand become more northeasterly temperatures will moderate as the \nday progresses. High temperatures on Sunday will generally range \nfrom mid 70s west of Lake Okeechobee to the upper 70s to around 80\nacross the rest of the region. \n\nDuring the early portion of next week, the latest global and \nensemble guidance is hinting at the potential for another mid level \nshortwave to dive down from the Southern Plains into the Lower \nMississippi Valley and along the Northern Gulf Coast heading into \nMonday and Tuesday. This mid level shortwave could cause \ncyclogenesis to take place at the surface along the Northwestern\nGulf coast early Monday before moving eastward across the Gulf \nand towards the Florida Peninsula through the early portion of \nnext week. Uncertainty in this part of the forecast remains on the\nhigher end as this is towards the end of the forecast period and \nthe latest guidance suite still remains in disagreement in regards\nto the intensity and timing of the surface low development along\nwith its eventual movement across the Gulf. However, with a \ngradual increase in moisture anticipated out ahead of this system,\nchances for showers for thunderstorms could increase across South\nFlorida during the early week period. The latest forecast takes a\nblend of the models and increases the chances of showers and \nthunderstorms during this time frame. This will continue to be \nmonitored as the week progresses.",
